00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:06.240
In order to implement linear regression, the first key step is for us to define something

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.260
called a cost function.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.760
This is something we'll build in this video.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.800
And the cost function will tell us how well the model is doing so that we can try to

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.640
get it to do better.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.560
Let's look at what this means.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:25.080
Recall that you have a training set that contains input features x and output targets

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.480
y.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:33.600
And the model you're going to use to fit this training set is this linear function f wb of

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.200
x equals w times x plus b.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.720
To introduce a little bit more terminology, the w and b are called the parameters of the

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.720
model.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.960
In machine learning, parameters of a model are the variables you can adjust during training

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.400
in order to improve the model.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:02.520
Sometimes you also hear the parameters w and b referred to as coefficients or as weights.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.600
Now let's take a look at what these parameters w and b do.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.580
Depending on the values you've chosen for w and b, you get a different function f of

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.520
x, which generates a different line on the graph.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:24.760
Remember that we can write f of x as a shorthand for f wb of x.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.640
We're going to take a look at some plots of f of x on a chart.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.080
Maybe you're already familiar with drawing lines on charts, but even if this is a review

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.800
for you, I hope this will help you build intuition on how w and b, the parameters,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.600
determine f.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:50.920
When w is equal to 0 and b is equal to 1.5, then f looks like this horizontal line.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:02:00.520
In this case, the function f of x is 0 times x plus 1.5, so f is always a constant value.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.880
It always predicts 1.5 for the estimated value of y.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.000
So y hat is always equal to b.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.640
And here, b is also called the y-interset because that's where it crosses the vertical

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.600
axis or the y-axis on this graph.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:28.840
As a second example, if w is 0.5 and b is equal to 0, then f of x is 0.5 times x.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.440
When x is 0, the prediction is also 0.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:38.400
And when x is 2, then the prediction is 0.5 times 2, which is 1.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.800
So you get a line that looks like this.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.440
And notice that the slope is 0.5 divided by 1.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.360
So the value of w gives you the slope of the line, which is 0.5.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:04.600
And finally, if w equals 0.5 and b equals 1, then f of x is 0.5 times x plus 1.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.640
And when x is 0, then f of x equals b, which is 1.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:15.080
So the line intersects the vertical axis at b, the y-intercept.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.600
Also when x is 2, then f of x is 2.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.040
So the line looks like this.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.160
Again, the slope is 0.5 divided by 1, so the value of w gives you the slope, which

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.320
is 0.5.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.760
Recall that you have a training set, like the one shown here.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.800
With linear regression, what you want to do is to choose values for the parameters w and

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.360
b so that the straight line you get from the function f somehow fits the data well,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.160
like maybe this line shown here.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.640
And when I say that the line fits the data visually, you can think of this to mean that

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:59.240
the line defined by f is roughly passing through or somewhat close to the training example

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.640
as compared to other possible lines that are not as close to these points.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:13.000
And just to remind you of some notation, a training example like this point here is defined by

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:20.640
x superstrip i, y superstrip i, where y is the target.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:30.280
For a given input xi, the function f also makes a predicted value for y, and the value

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.080
that it predicts for y is y-hat i, shown here.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:41.320
For our choice of a model, f of xi is w times xi plus b.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:50.560
Stated differently, the prediction y-hat i is f of wb of xi, where for the model where

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:59.670
using f of xi is equal to w xi plus b.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:07.670
So now the question is, how do you find values for w and b so that the prediction y-hat i

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:17.430
is close to the true target yi for many or maybe all training examples xi yi?

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:22.950
To answer that question, let's first take a look at how to measure how well a line fits

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.950
the training data.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.970
To do that, we're going to construct our cost function.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:36.710
The cost function takes the prediction y-hat and compares it to the target y by taking

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.790
y-hat minus y.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.830
This difference is called the error.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.150
We're measuring how far off the prediction is from the target.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:52.110
Next, let's compute the square of this error.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:57.810
Also, we're going to want to compute this term for different training examples i in the

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.310
training set.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:05.430
So when measuring the error, for example, i, we'll compute this squared error term.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.230
Finally, we want to measure the error across the entire training set.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.990
In particular, let's sum up the squared errors like this.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:20.190
We'll sum from i equals 1, 2, 3, all the way up to m.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:26.190
And remember that m is the number of training examples, which is 47 for this data set.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:31.230
Notice that if we have more training examples, m is larger, and your cost function will

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:35.830
calculate a bigger number since it's summing over more examples.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:40.830
So to build a cost function that doesn't automatically get bigger as the training set

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:49.050
size gets larger, by convention, we will compute the average squared error instead of the total

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.050
squared error.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.190
And we do that by dividing by m like this.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.410
Okay, we're nearly there.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.150
Just one last thing.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:05.350
By convention, the cost function that machine learning people use actually divides by 2

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:06.350
times m.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:11.790
The extra division by 2 is just meant to make some of our later calculations a little bit

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:17.110
neater, but the cost function still works whether you include this division by 2 or

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:18.110
not.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:23.110
So this expression right here is the cost function, and we're going to write j of

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:28.290
wb to refer to the cost function.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.230
This is also called the squared error cost function.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:37.350
And it's called this because you're taking the square of these error terms.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:42.350
In machine learning, different people will use different cost functions for different

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.350
applications.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:50.030
But the squared error cost function is by far the most commonly used one for linear regression.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:54.630
And for that matter, for all regression problems, where it seems to give good results for

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.710
many applications.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:05.670
So just as a reminder, the prediction y hat is equal to the output of the model f at x.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:15.910
So we can rewrite the cost function j of wb as 1 over 2m times the sum from i equals 1

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:23.030
to m of f of xi minus yi, the quantity squared.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:28.630
Eventually, we're going to want to find values of w and b that make the cost function

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.230
small.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:37.070
But before going there, let's first gain more intuition about what j of wb is really

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.510
computing.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:43.150
At this point, you might be thinking, we've done a whole lot of math to define the cost

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:44.250
function.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.750
But what exactly is it doing?

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:51.310
Let's go on to the next video, where we'll step through one example of what the cost

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:56.150
function is really computing, that I hope will help you build intuition about what it

00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:01.950
means if j of wb is large versus if the cost j is small.

